      "created_at": "2016-12-09T22:13:53Z",
      "updated_at": "2016-12-09T22:35:48Z",
      "body": "One last word to the general topic of SSL on your local network (aussuming the Pi-Hole web UI not visible from the web):\r\n\r\nWhether you need encryption or not strongly depends on your local feeling of endangerment. If you have bad guys on your network, then you will need encryption as e.g. [ARP spoofing](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/ARP_spoofing) is a feasible attack.\r\n\r\nAs has been mentioned by @Mcat12 LE is not an option here.\r\n\r\nSelf-signed certificates are not an option either because people **will** add exception rules in their browser for these certificates to remove the annoying warnings. But this demolishes the entire point of having certificates in the first place, specifically, that not anybody can just make up a certificate and then have other computers believe that they're the _right_ server. Everything about SSL/TLS really requires having trustworthy authorities which sign unique certificates.\r\nIf you have set the exception for `pi.hole` everyone else in the network can pretend that they are the *real* `pi.hole`, capture your traffic and fiddle with it - see my mentioning about ARP spoofing above which can be used to start a Man-In-The-Middle-attack.\r\n\r\nComing back to my initial point: If you are certain that you don't have bad guys in your network, then sending everything in the clear is just fine on your network. Of course, this also includes having a strong WiFi encryption with a strong password.",
      "author_association": "MEMBER",
      "pin": null,
